{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["Now behold, it says: <b>And on the seventh day there is a solemn assembly (עֲצֶרֶת) for HaShem your Elohim; you shall do no work</b>, yet it did not mention the first day with regard to the prohibition of labor. Likewise, it did not mention the prohibition of labor on the Festival of Weeks, nor on Sukkot, nor did it mention the commandment of the lulav, and similarly it did not mention here the Day of Remembrance and the tenth of the month. The reason is as I explained (Ramban on Deuteronomy 16:1): for now it comes only to mention the appearance (at the Temple) and the rejoicing — that all Israel are obligated to go on these three pilgrimage festivals to the place that HaShem will choose, and to celebrate there, each man according to the gift of his hand, according to the blessing of HaShem that He has given him, to rejoice before Him, to give thanks for all the goodness that He has bestowed upon us in His mercy and in the abundance of His kindness. And He added, \"and on the seventh day there is a solemn assembly,\" its meaning being: six days you shall eat unleavened bread, and on the seventh day there is still upon them a solemn assembly. The reason is that you are still detained before Him to eat the unleavened bread. And since He said עֲצֶרֶת הִיא (it is a solemn assembly), and it would be understood to mean being detained only for eating unleavened bread like the days before it, it was necessary to warn: you shall do no work. Similarly, He said regarding the eighth day of the Festival of Sukkot in the section of the appointed times that is in the Torah of the Priests (Leviticus 23:36): \"Seven days you shall bring an offering made by fire to HaShem; on the eighth day there shall be a holy convocation, and you shall bring an offering made by fire to HaShem; it is a solemn assembly (עֲצֶרֶת), you shall do no manner of servile work\" — to say that it is detained upon the first days in the bringing of the fire-offering, and it is still prohibited in the doing of servile work. And by way of truth, עֲצֶרֶת is Knesset Yisrael, and it comes to teach us the secret of the day. But it is not equivalent to the עֲצֶרֶת of the eighth day, for the eighth day is a festival in its own right, and its offering is that of Knesset Yisrael, and it is not like the offerings of the seven days; but the seventh day of Passover is part of the totality of the festival. And עֲצֶרֶת — sometimes it is counted as an eighth and sometimes it is included within. And behold, it is like the Sabbath, in which there is זָכוֹר (remember) and שָׁמוֹר (observe) — the honor of the day and the honor of the night. And the offering on the seventh day is part of the totality of the festival. Therefore it says here, עֲצֶרֶת לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ לֹא תַעֲשֶׂה מְלָאכָה (a solemn assembly for HaShem your Elohim; you shall do no work), and there it says, וְהִקְרַבְתֶּם אִשֶּׁה לַה׳ עֲצֶרֶת הִיא (you shall bring an offering made by fire to HaShem; it is a solemn assembly), for the fire-offering is the עֲצֶרֶת, and therefore it is a small feast, and the enlightened will understand. And it did not mention here the eighth day of the festival, for it was already mentioned, and it does not obligate us to go up on it for the festival another time."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "In the phrase 'and it does not obligate us to go up on it for the festival another time,' the subject is left unstated; the context refers to the obligation of רְאִיָּה (pilgrim appearance) discussed throughout."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:08.268377399Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}